# Defect-Rich Graphene Nanomesh Produced by Thermal Exfoliation of Metal-Organic Frameworks for the Oxygen Reduction Reaction

## abstract
Although graphene nanomesh is an attractive 2D carbon material, general synthetic routes to produce functional graphene nanomesh in large-scale are complex and tedious. Herein, we elaborately design a simple two-step dimensional reduction strategy for exploring nitrogen-doped graphene nanomesh by thermal exfoliation of crystal- and shape-modified metal-organic frameworks (MOFs). MOF nanoleaves with 2D rather than 3D crystal structure are used as the precursor, which are further thermally unraveled into nitrogen-doped graphene nanomesh by using metal chlorides as the exfoliators and etching agent. The nitrogen-doped graphene nanomesh has a unique ultrathin two-dimensional morphology, high porosity, rich and accessible nitrogen-doped active sites, and defective graphene edges, contributing to an unprecedented catalytic activity for the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) in acid electrolytes. This approach is suitable for scalable production.

Among two-dimensional (2D) materials,[1] graphene nanomesh (GM), that is, porous graphene, is a relatively new concept of two-dimensional single- or few-layer of carbon nanosheet with in-plane nanopores.[2] In contrast to perfect graphene nanosheets which are prone to aggregate, GMs have increased specific surface areas and accessible vertical pathway between the layers, which expand the application of GM in energy related systems.[3] For example, its numerous nanoholes endow GMs with lots of defective edges, which probably provide extra active sites for catalyzing the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR).[4] To date, the most convenient method to produce large-scale GM is pore engineering of reduced graphene oxide (GO) to create nanopores and defects in the basal planes, including chemical activation of graphite oxide.[5] Therefore, a general route to produce catalytic active GM in large-scale will include preparation of GO precursor (e.g., via chemical exfoliation of graphite), pore engineering of GO, and post-doping of heteroatoms, which is quite tedious and lack of novelty.
Recently, one-step carbonization of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)[6] have become the most convenient method for preparing functional heteroatoms- and/or metal-doped carbon.[7] Is it possible to prepare functional GM in large scale via simply carbonization of easily obtained MOF precursor? Although it was considered as an advantage that MOFderived carbon would inherent the shape and porous characteristic from MOF parent, we find it becomes a bottleneck to prepare MOF-derived carbons with low dimensions. Because most MOFs that can be prepared under mild conditions via simple processes have three-dimensional (3D) crystal structure and shape. Recently, Xus group synthesized rod-shape MOF-74 instead of the common microcrystalline MOF-74, by addition a modulator (salicylic acid).[8] They then prepared one-dimensional carbon nanorods by thermal transform of rod-shaped MOF-74.[8] Thus breaking the dimensional limitation of traditional 3D MOFs probably paves the way to prepare novel low-dimensional MOF-derived carbon.
As a representative example, we find that the variety of zeolite imidazolate framework (ZIF) nanoparticles can be expanded to ZIF nanoleaves with 2D crystal structures and 2D shape by simply replacing the methanolic solvent with water. In detail, 2-methylimidazole served as a bidentate bridging ligand that uses two $\mathbf{N}$ atoms to coordinate with metal ions to form zigzag chain units (Scheme 1). Each chain is hydrogen bonded to form an overall 2D supramolecular frameworks.[9a] Inspired by the unique layered structure and N-containing organic links of ZIF nanoleaves, we attempt to unravel the impact layers for preparing N-doped graphene nanomesh (NGM).
Herein, ultrathin NGM was fabricated by a two-step dimensional-reduction strategy. Zn-containing ZIF nanoleaves (Zn-ZIF-L) with 2D crystal structure rather than regular ZIF with 3D structure was first selected as the precursor. Then $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-L was further exfoliated into ultrathin 2D NGM under inert atmosphere by using metal chlorides as the exfoliators and etching agents. The best NGM is thin $(1.3\mathrm{nm})$ , has a high specific surface area $(1329.5\mathbf{m}^{2}\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ , and nitrogen doping $(4.68\mathrm{at\%})$ as well as abundant hierarchical pores. Thanks to the overall structural and compositional advantages, NGM exhibits an unprecedented catalytic activity for ORR in acid electrolyte, which is important for metal-free carbon-based electrocatalysis for ORR acidic systems.
$Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-L was prepared by using zinc nitrite and 2- methylimidazole precursors in aqueous solution. Zn-ZIF-L shows a leaf-like shape, a thickness of approximately $160\mathrm{nm}$ , and a lateral dimensional size of about $5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Figure S1a in the Supporting Information). The layered crystal structure of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-L is shown schematically in Scheme 1 based on the experimental $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) pattern, which matches well with simulated XRD pattern of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-L (Figure S1b).[9] Direct carbonization of $Z\mathrm{n-ZIF-L}$ at typical temperature of $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ produces a leaf-like nitrogen-doped porous carbon (NC-L-800) with a slightly distorted shape (Figure S2) compared with the original Zn-ZIF-L precursor (Figure S1a). To unravel the impact crystal layers of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIFL and produce $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-L derived 2D ultrathin porous carbon, exfoliator and etching agent of LiCl/KCl was used and mixed with $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-L precursors adequately (Scheme 1). Then, the mixture was heat treated under the protection of nitrogen atmosphere from room temperature to maximum $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to produce nitrogen-doped graphene nanomesh (NGM). As monitored by the TGA-DSC analysis (Figure S3a), the mixture shows a pronounced endothermic process at $369^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ which corresponds to the melting of LiCl/KCl.[10] Another two endothermic process at $509^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $684^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ might due to the slight compositional change of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-L.[11] Although the weight of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-L might change a bit, the molten metal chlorides are quite stable below $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .[12] Therefore, the total weight seems constant below $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ considering that the content of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-L is only about $2\mathrm{wt\%}$ in the mixture. After the temperature is increased to more than $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , the mixture starts to lose weight accompanying with a clear endothermic peak at $859^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , which might be due to the strong interaction between $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-L and LiCl/KCl accompanied with exfoliation, carbonization, and volatile of decomposed chemicals. In addition, vaporization of LiCl/KCl molten salts also happens when the temperature reaches to $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}^{[12]}$ and contribute to the fast weight loss. In contrast, pyrolysis of individual $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-L shows a totally different TG/DSC curve that shows much simpler process (Figure S3b). Based on the TGA-DSC analysis and the morphology observation of $Z\mathrm{n}\cdot$ - ZIF-L before (Figure S1a) and after (Figure S4a,b) thermal treatment, we propose the following reaction process (Scheme 1): When the LiCl/KCl is molten $(>369^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , the small metal ions would insert into the crystal layers. Then, the exfoliation, carbonization, and evaporation would happen simultaneously at the higher temperatures $\mathrm{(>700^{\circ}C)}$ . After cooling down, the 2D ultrathin nitrogen-doped porous carbon nanosheets contain metal salts and other impurities (Figure S4a). These impurities are proved to be $\mathrm{Li}_{x}\mathrm C_{y}$ and $\mathrm{LiKCO}_{3}$ according to the XRD patterns (Figure S4c), but they can be fully removed by washing with acid. Finally, the product of nitrogen-doped graphene nanomesh (NGM) was obtained (Figure S4b). Traditional $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF polyhedron with 3D crystal structure (Figure S5a,b) was also thermally treated by the same process. However, irregular carbon materials without sheet-like morphology were obtained (Figure S5c,d). Even though metal ions $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ and $\mathbf{K}^{+}$ ) inserted into the cavities of 3D $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF, it is difficult to unravel the interconnected 3D crystal structure and convert it into 2D layered carbons.
As observed from the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image, NGM prepared at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (termed NGM-800) is composed of ultrathin nanosheets with lateral sizes reaching up to a few micrometers (Figure 1a,b and Figure S6a). Massive nanopores can be observed clearly on the surface of NGM nanosheet (Figure 1c and Figure S6b,c). As shown in Figure 1d and Figure S7a, transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images also show a transparent nanosheet, indicating the quite thin NGM. The thickness of NGM was carefully estimated by atomic force microscope (AFM), which is approximately $1.3\:\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 1 e). Furthermore, high-resolution TEM image reveals that NGM mainly consists of a few layered graphene sheets (“defective” graphitic carbon) as well as nanoporous structure (Figure 1 f and Figure S7b,c). Metal elements (e.g. K, Li, $Z\mathrm{n}{\dot{}}$ ) and other impurities (e.g. Cl) is hardly detected by EDX (Figure S8a). Elemental mappings of NGM clearly show the homogeneously distributed nitrogen, carbon, and oxygen (Figure S8b).
For studying the influence of temperature on the structural properties of NGM, we conducted different thermal exfoliation at 700 to $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in case of excess weight loss. The corresponding samples were named as NGM- $x$ ( $x=700$ , 800, 900). According to the XRD analysis (Figure 2a), all the samples present a wide peak centered at around $21^{\circ}$ , which can be attributed to the diffraction of (002) plane of lowgraphitic carbon materials. However, the common (002) diffraction peak of amorphous carbon is usually observed at (Table S1),[14] which are adopted to provide quantitative evidence for the degree of structural disorder in the firstorder spectral region $(1000{-}2000\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ ). Normally, G band located at about $1580\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is known to be features of perfect graphitic lattice, and D1 band corresponds to the vibration mode of carbon atoms at the graphene edges. Generally, the disordered degree of carbon can be estimated by the relativity of integrated areas of D1 to G band $(I_{\mathrm{D1}}/I_{\mathrm{G}})$ . The calculated ratios of $I_{\mathrm{D1}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ are 2.17 for NC-L-800, 2.35 for NGM-700, 3.15 for NGM-800, and 3.19 for NGM-900. The results show that $I_{\mathrm{D1}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ increased after the extensive exfoliation at higher temperatures, indicating a much higher percentage of carbon atoms at the edges of graphene layer, which are regarded as probable active sites for catalysis.
