Similar to the trend tested in alkaline electrolyte (Figure S12–Figure S14), NGM-800 showed the highest catalytic activity with the most positive ORR peak located at $0.68\mathrm{V}$ in acidic electrolyte (Figure 4 a), which is much more positive than that of NGM-700 (0.44 V) and NGM-900 $(0.65\mathrm{V})$ . As
For considering real practical applications to fuel cell systems, metal-free catalysts with superior acidic ORR performance comparable to Pt are much desired. Dissolution of metal species really happens in practical harsh conditions (e.g., high temperature, long term), causing serious reduction of ORR performance. Although the state of art metal-free carbon-based catalyst have comparable and even better performance for catalyzing ORR than coma metal-free carbon-based electrocatalyst, it is surprised that NGM-800 displays an unprecedented ORR activity with an impressive $E_{\mathrm{onset}}$ of $0.860\mathrm{V}_{;}$ $E_{1/2}$ of $0.781\mathrm{V}_{:}$ and $J_{\mathrm{d}}$ of $4.243\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ (Figure $\boldsymbol{4}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{b}}$ ). Although the achieved performance is slightly inferior comparing to $20\mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ which displays $E_{\mathrm{onset}}=0.936\:\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{:}}$ $E_{1/2}=0.831\:\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{:}}$ $J_{\mathrm{d}}{=}5.246\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , it has the highest performance amongst metal-free carbonbased electrocatalysts reported to date (Table S5). In addition, kinetic current density $(J_{\mathrm{k}})$ of NGM-800 calculated from Figure 4 b is $38.12\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ at $0.72\mathrm{V}$ , which is close to that of $20\mathrm{wt\%}$ Pt/C $46.34\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ ) but is much higher than that of NGM-700 $(0.59\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2})$ , NGM-900 $(14.51\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2})$ ), and $\mathsf{N C-L-800}$ (ca. $0\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2},$ . The results demonstrate that porous ultrathin 2D structure significantly promotes the ORR kinetic process.
The electron transfer number $(n)$ for NGM-800 is calculated to be 3.85, and peroxide yield is below $7.6\%$ over potential range of $0.2\mathrm{-}0.8\:\mathrm{V}$ (Figure 4 c) based on the RRDE record in Figure S15. The results indicate that NGM-800 processed mainly a four-electron transfer pathway for ORR in acidic electrolyte, which was further demonstrated by Koutecky-Levich (K–L) plot derived from LSV curves (Figure S16). In addition, NGM-800 catalyst is also stable in the acidic electrolyte. After carrying out 5000 continuous CV cycles, NGM-800 shows a small negative shift of $E_{1/2}=23~\mathrm{mV}$ with almost no decay of diffusion limiting current density (Figure 4 d). In contrast, $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ showed a negative shift of $E_{1/2}=$ $36\mathrm{mV}$ and $3.6\%$ decay of diffusion limiting current density. The great catalytic of NGM-800 for acidic ORR are probably due to the ultrathin 2D morphology with hierarchical pores as well as defect-enriched structure. The unique low-dimensional and porous structure ensures the fast mass transport and sustainable exposure of active sites, and prevents the deactivation of active sites induced by the restacking of carbon nanosheets or the blocking of transfer pathways.
In summary, ultrathin nitrogen-doped graphene nanomesh (NGM) with outstanding electrocatalytic activity for ORR in acidic electrolyte was prepared by simple and efficient thermal exfoliation of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -containing zeolite imidazolate framework nanoleaves $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}$ -ZIF-L) under inert atmosphere by using the mixed KCl and LiCl as the exfoliators and etching agent. The effect of different metal chlorides on thermal exfoliation of $Z\mathrm{n-ZIF-L}$ was carefully investigated. We suppose that potassium ions are more effective in intercalation and exfoliation of $Z\mathrm{n-ZIF-L}$ derived carbon, whereas lithium ions might contribute more to etching the carbons to create larger mesopores. The NGM prepared shows unprecedented catalytic activity for ORR not only in alkaline but also in acid electrolytes as a result of its twodimensional morphology with a thickness of $1.3\mathrm{nm}$ , a high specific surface area of $1329.5\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , abundant nanopores and defect active sites. Breaking the dimensional limitation of tradition 3D MOF may pave the pathway to prepare thousands of novel low-dimensional MOF-derived functional carbon materials and improve the performance in various applications.
